.TH BASIC 1 "October 2026"
.SH NAME
basic \- extended integer BASIC interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B basic
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Basic
is an interactive BASIC interpreter written in strict K&R C for the AT&T 3B2
and other System V systems.
It is an
.I integer
BASIC: all numbers are signed 32-bit integers, and there is no floating point.
Strings of up to 80 characters, one-dimensional arrays,
subroutines, and file save and load are supported.
.PP
When started, the interpreter prints a banner and the prompt
.RB `` "? " ''.
A line that begins with a line number is stored in the program;
any other line is executed immediately.
Input is read from the standard input, so program text and commands
may also be piped in.
.PP
Keywords may be typed in upper or lower case.
Text outside double quotes is converted to upper case when a line is entered,
so
.B LIST
shows keywords in upper case and string literals exactly as typed.
.SH COMMANDS
These are recognized only at the prompt, not inside a program.
.TP 12
.B RUN
Clear all variables and run the program from its lowest line number.
The random number seed is not reset (see
.BR RND ).
.TP
.BI LIST " [n[\-m]]"
List the program.
.B LIST
lists every line,
.B "LIST 50"
lists line 50,
.B "LIST 50\-90"
lists lines 50 through 90,
.B "LIST 50\-"
lists from line 50 to the end, and
.B "LIST \-90"
lists up to line 90.
.TP
.B NEW
Delete the program and clear all variables.
.TP
.BI SAVE " ""file"""
Write the program to
.I file
as text, one numbered line per line.
Prints
.BR SAVED .
.TP
.BI LOAD " ""file"""
Delete the current program, then read numbered lines from
.IR file .
Prints
.BR LOADED .
The file name must be in double quotes.
.TP
.B BYE
Leave the interpreter.
End-of-file (usually CTRL-D) at the prompt does the same.
.PP
Typing a line number followed by nothing deletes that line.
Typing a line number followed by text inserts or replaces that line.
Line numbers may be any non-negative integer; lines are kept in order.
.SH "VARIABLES AND TYPES"
Variable names are a single letter.
There are 26 numeric variables
.BR A \- Z
and 26 string variables
.BR A$ \- Z$ .
Arrays are one-dimensional:
.BR A(n)
is numeric and
.BR A$(n)
is a string array.
Array subscripts run from 0 through the declared size.
An array that is used without being declared with
.B DIM
has subscripts 0 through 10.
Scalars and arrays of the same letter are separate variables
(for example
.B A
and
.BR A(1) ).
Numeric variables and arrays start at 0 and string variables at the empty string.
.PP
Numbers are signed 32-bit integers, from \-2147483648 to 2147483647.
Arithmetic that overflows wraps around silently.
To write the smallest value, use
.BR "\-2147483647\-1" .
Strings hold at most 80 characters; longer results are silently truncated.
.SH EXPRESSIONS
Operators, from highest precedence to lowest:
.PP
.nf
    ^                       exponentiation (right to left)
    unary \-  unary +
    *  /  MOD               multiply, divide, remainder
    +  \-                    add, subtract
    =  <>  <  >  <=  >=     comparison
    NOT
    AND
    OR
.fi
.PP
Division truncates toward zero.
.B MOD
takes the sign of its left operand.
A negative exponent yields 0 (1 if the base is 1).
Division or
.B MOD
by zero is an error.
Because unary minus ranks below
.BR ^ ,
.B \-2^2
is \-4.
.PP
Comparisons yield \-1 for true and 0 for false.
.BR AND ,
.BR OR ,
and
.B NOT
operate bitwise on the 32-bit values, as in Microsoft BASIC.
This means
.B NOT 5
is \-6, which is non-zero and therefore true when tested by
.BR IF .
.PP
Strings may be joined with
.BR + .
Two strings may be compared with
.BR = ,
.BR <> ,
.BR < ,
.BR > ,
.BR <= ,
and
.BR >= ;
the comparison is by character code.
Mixing strings and numbers in one expression is a
.B TYPE MISMATCH
error.
.SH STATEMENTS
Several statements may be placed on one line, separated by colons.
.TP
.B [LET] \fIvar\fP = \fIexpr\fP
Assignment.
.B LET
is optional.
.TP
.B PRINT \fIlist\fP
Print the items in
.IR list ,
which are expressions separated by semicolons or commas.
.B ?
may be used in place of
.BR PRINT .
A semicolon prints the next item immediately after the previous one.
A comma moves to the next 14-column print zone.
A trailing semicolon or comma suppresses the final newline.
Numbers are printed with no leading or trailing blanks.
.TP
.B "INPUT [""prompt""; or ,] \fIvar\fP[,\fIvar\fP...]"
Read values from the terminal.
With a semicolon after the prompt, or no prompt, the prompt is followed by
.RB `` "? "''.
With a comma after the prompt, no question mark is printed.
Several variables may be answered on one line separated by commas;
if too few values are given,
.RB `` "?? "''
asks for the rest.
A non-numeric answer to a numeric variable prints
.B ?REDO FROM START
and the whole
.B INPUT
is asked again.
An empty numeric answer is taken as 0.
End-of-file stops the program.
.TP
.B "IF \fIcond\fP THEN \fIstatement\fP [ELSE \fIstatement\fP]"
.TP
.B "IF \fIcond\fP THEN \fIline\fP [ELSE \fIline\fP]"
.TP
.B "IF \fIcond\fP GOTO \fIline\fP"
Conditional execution.
.I Cond
is true if non-zero.
A line number after
.B THEN
or
.B ELSE
means
.BR GOTO .
Statements following the chosen branch on the same line, separated by colons,
are executed after it.
.TP
.B "GOTO \fIexpr\fP"
.TP
.B "GOSUB \fIexpr\fP"
Jump to, or call as a subroutine, the line whose number is the value of
.IR expr .
Computed targets such as
.B GOTO 100*N
are allowed.
Typing
.B GOTO
at the prompt starts the program at that line.
.TP
.B RETURN
Return from the most recent
.BR GOSUB .
.TP
.B "FOR \fIv\fP = \fIa\fP TO \fIb\fP [STEP \fIc\fP]"
.TP
.B "NEXT [\fIv\fP[,\fIv\fP...]]"
Counting loop.
The step defaults to 1 and may be negative.
The body always executes at least once, even if the starting value is already
past the limit, as in classic Microsoft BASIC.
Restarting a
.B FOR
on a variable that is already active discards that loop and any loops inside it.
.TP
.B "DIM \fIA\fP(\fIn\fP)[,\fIB$\fP(\fIn\fP)...]"
Declare arrays with subscripts 0 through
.IR n .
Numeric arrays may be up to size 5000 and string arrays up to 500.
Declaring an existing array again discards its contents.
.TP
.B "DATA \fIitem\fP[,\fIitem\fP...]"
.TP
.B "READ \fIvar\fP[,\fIvar\fP...]"
.TP
.B RESTORE
.B DATA
holds numbers and quoted strings.
.B READ
takes the next items in program order, and
.B RESTORE
returns to the first item.
Reading past the end is an
.B OUT OF DATA
error, and reading a string into a numeric variable or the reverse is a
.B TYPE MISMATCH
error.
.TP
.B "REM \fItext\fP"
.TP
.B "' \fItext\fP"
A comment.
Everything to the end of the line is ignored, colons included.
.TP
.B RANDOMIZE [\fIn\fP]
Set the random number seed to
.IR n ,
or to the time of day if
.I n
is omitted.
.TP
.B CLS
Send a form feed to the terminal.
.TP
.B END
Stop the program.
.TP
.B STOP
Stop the program and print
.BR "BREAK IN \fIline\fP" .
.PP
.BR GOSUB ,
.BR RETURN ,
.BR FOR ,
and
.B NEXT
may be used only inside a running program.
At the prompt they produce
.BR "?ILLEGAL DIRECT" .
.SH FUNCTIONS
.TP 14
.BI RND( n )
A pseudo-random integer from 1 through
.IR n .
.I n
must be at least 1.
The generator starts from a fixed seed every time
.I basic
is started, so a program that does not call
.B RANDOMIZE
produces the same sequence each time.
The seed carries over from one
.B RUN
to the next within a session.
.TP
.BI SQR( n )
Integer square root, rounded down.
An error if
.I n
is negative.
.TP
.BI ABS( n )
Absolute value.
.TP
.BI SGN( n )
\-1, 0, or 1 according to the sign of
.IR n .
.TP
.BI INT( n )
Returns
.IR n .
It is provided for compatibility, since all values are already integers.
.TP
.BI LEN( s$ )
Length of a string.
.TP
.BI ASC( s$ )
Character code of the first character, or 0 for an empty string.
.TP
.BI VAL( s$ )
The integer at the start of the string; 0 if there is none.
.TP
.BI CHR$( n )
A one-character string whose code is the low eight bits of
.IR n .
.TP
.BI STR$( n )
The number as a string, with no leading blank.
.TP
.BI LEFT$( s$,n )
The first
.I n
characters.
.TP
.BI RIGHT$( s$,n )
The last
.I n
characters.
.TP
.BI MID$( s$,start [, len ])
.I Len
characters starting at position
.IR start ,
where the first character is position 1.
If
.I len
is omitted, the rest of the string is returned.
.TP
.BI SPACE$( n )
A string of
.I n
blanks, 0 through 80.
.SH "INTERRUPTING"
The interrupt key (usually DEL or CTRL-C) stops a running program, a
.B LIST
in progress, and prints
.BR "BREAK IN \fIline\fP" .
At the prompt it simply prints a new line and prompts again.
Variables are kept, so the program can be examined or run again.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Errors are reported as
.B ?MESSAGE
and, when they occur in a running program,
.B ?MESSAGE IN \fIline\fP.
A running program stops at the first error; the interpreter returns to the prompt.
.TP 28
.B SYNTAX ERROR
The statement or expression could not be understood,
including a number with a decimal point.
.TP
.B TYPE MISMATCH
A string was used where a number is needed, or the reverse.
.TP
.B ILLEGAL FUNCTION CALL
An argument is out of range:
.B RND
below 1,
.B SQR
of a negative number,
.B MID$
with a start below 1 or a negative length,
.B LEFT$
or
.B RIGHT$
with a negative count, or
.B SPACE$
outside 0 to 80.
.TP
.B ILLEGAL DIRECT
A statement that works only in a program was typed at the prompt.
.TP
.B DIVISION BY ZERO
Division or
.B MOD
by zero.
.TP
.B SUBSCRIPT OUT OF RANGE
An array index is negative or larger than the array size.
.TP
.B BAD SUBSCRIPT
A
.B DIM
size is negative or exceeds the limit.
.TP
.B OUT OF MEMORY
An array or program line could not be allocated.
.TP
.B MISSING ( \fRor\fP MISSING )
A function call or parenthesized expression is not balanced.
.TP
.B UNDEFINED LINE \fIn\fP
A
.B GOTO
or
.B GOSUB
refers to a line that does not exist.
.TP
.B RETURN WITHOUT GOSUB
.TP
.B NEXT WITHOUT FOR
Control structures are not matched.
.TP
.B GOSUB NESTING TOO DEEP
.TP
.B FOR NESTING TOO DEEP
More than 32 active
.BR GOSUB s
or more than 16 active
.BR FOR s.
.TP
.B OUT OF DATA
.B READ
ran past the last
.BR DATA .
.PP
These messages are printed by the commands and are not program errors:
.B ?NEED QUOTED NAME
(a
.B SAVE
or
.B LOAD
without a file name in quotes),
.B ?CANNOT OPEN \fIname\fP
(the file could not be opened),
.B ?PROGRAM TOO LONG
(more than 1000 lines), and
.BR "?REDO FROM START" ,
described under
.BR INPUT .
.SH LIMITS
.nf
    Program lines                    1000
    Characters in an input line      255
    Characters in a string           80
    Numeric array subscripts         0 to 5000
    String array subscripts          0 to 500
    Active FOR loops                 16
    Active GOSUBs                    32
    Integer range                    \-2147483648 to 2147483647
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
A program that prints the squares of 1 through 5:
.PP
.nf
    10 FOR I = 1 TO 5
    20 PRINT I; " SQUARED IS "; I*I
    30 NEXT I
    RUN
.fi
.PP
A subroutine called twice:
.PP
.nf
    10 A = 3: GOSUB 100
    20 A = 4: GOSUB 100
    30 END
    100 PRINT "SQR("; A; ") = "; SQR(A)
    110 RETURN
.fi
.PP
Prompting for a string and a number:
.PP
.nf
    10 INPUT "Name"; N$
    20 INPUT "How many"; K
    30 FOR I = 1 TO K: PRINT "HELLO, "; N$: NEXT I
.fi
.PP
Saving and reloading a program:
.PP
.nf
    SAVE "squares"
    NEW
    LOAD "squares"
.fi
.PP
Running a program from a shell script:
.PP
.nf
    (cat squares.bas; echo RUN) | basic
.fi
.SH "DIFFERENCES FROM MICROSOFT BASIC"
There is no floating point: a decimal point is a syntax error,
.B /
truncates,
and functions such as
.B SIN
and
.B LOG
do not exist.
There are no multi-dimensional arrays, no
.BR TAB ,
no
.BR INSTR ,
no
.BR "ON \fIexpr\fP GOTO" ,
no user-defined functions, and no multi-letter variable names.
.B RND
takes a limit and returns an integer rather than a fraction.
.B ^
associates from right to left, so
.B 2^3^2
is 512.
.SH BUGS
Numbers outside the 32-bit range wrap silently when typed in answer to
.BR INPUT ,
given to
.BR VAL ,
or written as a literal in a program.
.PP
Redeclaring an array with
.B DIM
is not reported as an error.
.PP
.B CHR$
does not report an out-of-range argument.
.PP
.B PRINT
prints the items before a syntax error in the same statement.
.PP
With the standard System V terminal settings the characters
.B @
and
.B #
are the line-kill and erase characters and cannot be typed as ordinary input
without a preceding backslash.
.SH FILES
.TP 12
.I file
Text files created by
.B SAVE
and read by
.BR LOAD ,
in the current directory unless a path is given.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR cc (1),
.IR stty (1)
